  "ssml": "<speak version=\"1.0\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis\" xmlns:mstts=\"https://www.w3.org/2001/mstts\" xml:lang=\"en-PH\">\n  <voice name=\"en-PH-JamesNeural\">\n    <mstts:express-as style=\"calm\">\n      <prosody rate=\"92%\" pitch=\"+1%\">\n        Alright, <break time=\"300ms\"/> Kuya James here with a quick rule recap. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Whenever you face an <phoneme alphabet=\"ipa\" ph=\"əˈnælədʒi\">analogy</phoneme> question, <break time=\"300ms\"/> check these five patterns in order. <break time=\"550ms\"/> First, <break time=\"300ms\"/> <emphasis level=\"moderate\">Antonyms</emphasis> for opposites. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Second, <break time=\"300ms\"/> <emphasis level=\"moderate\">Synonyms</emphasis> for twin meanings. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Third, <break time=\"300ms\"/> <emphasis level=\"moderate\">Cause and Effect</emphasis>. <break time=\"550ms\"/> Fourth, <break time=\"300ms\"/> <emphasis level=\"moderate\">Part to Whole</emphasis>. <break time=\"550ms\"/> And fifth, <break time=\"300ms\"/> <emphasis level=\"moderate\">Location</emphasis>, <break time=\"300ms\"/> like buying sweet durian at a <phoneme alphabet=\"ipa\" ph=\"ˈmɑːti\">Mati</phoneme> fruit stand, <break time=\"300ms\"/> or riding a jeepney to school.\n      </prosody>\n    </mstts:express-as>\n  </voice>\n</speak>",
